From c71182451193d3e688ac32945b905022329849d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 00:47:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(client): the search keeps its work a thread: one set of
 tables, each board's mobility once

A search clears what it reads before reading it (ttFlag gates the table; the
move lists are written before they are read) and runs start to finish without
yielding, so the searches of one thread share one set of tables instead of
allocating and zeroing 3.3 MB for every think, assessment and review position.

The pieces' room to move, a third of the search's time, is a property of the
board alone, and the search meets the same board again and again (the next
iteration's frontier, a re-search after a null window or a reduction, captures
in another order): it is kept by the board's hash (the side to move taken
out, so a null move's board is met too), emptied at each search's start, in
a 64K-slot table a thread. 36% of the calls hit on the bench's positions.
Nodes, depths and moves are unchanged (EXPECTED_SEARCH).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011SfWVyoMjkEfNUzxfLRC8S
